const DOTS_FA1A = {
h1:{fa:"fa1",iq:0,v:"Analyse",t:"Analyse the current health status of Australians using Australia's Health and other health reports, tables and graphs",
inc:["What does the data show?","Major causes of morbidity and mortality and life expectancy for males, females and the total population","Sociological causes of risky health behaviours","Where do inequities exist and what can be done about them?"],
big:"Australians are, on average, among the healthiest people on Earth — but that average hides big gaps, and most of our burden now comes from preventable chronic disease.",
notes:[
["Reading health status like a marker","Health status is measured with **epidemiology**: the study of the patterns and causes of health and disease in populations. The key measures are **life expectancy**, **mortality** (death rates), **morbidity** (illness rates), **infant mortality** and **burden of disease** (DALYs — years of healthy life lost to early death or illness). When you 'analyse', you don't just quote a number. You identify the trend, explain the relationship behind it and draw out the implication."],
["What the data shows","Life expectancy at birth is about **81 years for males and 85 years for females** (AIHW, 2020–22), roughly 83 overall. Over the last century it has risen by more than 20 years, driven by lower infant mortality, vaccination, safer workplaces and roads, and better treatment of heart disease. Mortality rates have fallen for most causes. The biggest killers are now **chronic diseases**: coronary heart disease and dementia lead, followed by stroke, lung cancer and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Dementia is the leading cause of death for females; coronary heart disease is the leading cause for males."],
["Morbidity: living longer, but not always well","About **half of Australians have at least one chronic condition**, and many have two or more (multimorbidity). The largest contributors to burden of disease are **cancer, musculoskeletal conditions, cardiovascular disease and mental and substance use disorders**. Mental health conditions are the leading cause of burden for young people. Around **38% of the total burden is preventable**, because it is linked to modifiable risk factors such as tobacco, overweight and obesity, poor diet, alcohol, physical inactivity and high blood pressure."],
["Sociological causes of risky behaviour","Risky behaviour is not just 'bad choices'. It is shaped by **social determinants**: income and education, peer and family norms, advertising and marketing, how close you live to fast food or alcohol outlets, cultural expectations (for example around masculinity and help-seeking), and stress. These help explain why men die younger (more risk-taking, later help-seeking, more dangerous jobs) and why smoking and obesity are concentrated in lower socioeconomic groups."],
["Where are the inequities?","Inequity means a difference in health that is **unfair and avoidable**. The gaps are biggest for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people in low socioeconomic areas, people in rural and remote areas, people with disability, and some culturally and linguistically diverse groups. Closing them needs action on the **determinants**, not just treatment: education, housing, employment, culturally safe services, and community-controlled health care."]],
con:[["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person born today can expect to live if current death rates stay the same.","≈81 years (males), ≈85 years (females)"],["Morbidity","The rate of illness or disease in a population, usually shown as prevalence or incidence.","About 1 in 2 Australians has a chronic condition"],["Mortality","The rate of death in a population over a set period, often age-standardised so groups can be compared.","Coronary heart disease is the leading single cause for males"],["Burden of disease (DALY)","Disability-adjusted life years: healthy life lost to early death (YLL) plus years lived with illness (YLD).","Cancer contributes the most burden of any disease group"],["Modifiable risk factor","A behaviour or condition that can be changed to reduce the risk of disease.","Tobacco use is the leading single risk factor for burden"]],
flow:["Social determinants","Risky behaviours","Chronic disease","Morbidity & mortality","Inequity"],
sum:["Australia has high life expectancy (≈81 for males, ≈85 for females) and it is still slowly rising.","The main causes of death are chronic diseases: coronary heart disease, dementia, stroke, lung cancer and COPD.","About half of Australians have a chronic condition; around 38% of the burden of disease is preventable.","Risky behaviours have sociological causes such as income, education, peers, marketing, culture and place.","Inequities are largest for First Nations, low-SES, rural/remote and disability groups. Fix them through the determinants."],
tip:"'Analyse' means identify the components AND show the relationships between them. Don't list statistics. Link each trend to a cause (a determinant or risk factor) and an implication (who it affects and what it means for the health system).",
reel:["h:Australians live *longer* than almost anyone… so why are we still getting sick?","s:≈83|years life expectancy, among the best in the OECD","p:Men vs women|Women live about 4 years longer. Men take more risks, work more dangerous jobs and see doctors later.","l:Top killers today|Coronary heart disease;Dementia;Stroke;Lung cancer;COPD","s:38%|of our disease burden is PREVENTABLE","p:Behaviour isn't random|Income, education, mates, marketing and where you live all shape risky choices.","r:Trend → Cause → Who's affected → Implication"],
mcq:[["Which measure combines years of life lost to early death with years lived with illness?",["Life expectancy","Disability-adjusted life years (DALYs)","Infant mortality rate","Incidence"],1,"A DALY = YLL + YLD, one year of healthy life lost. It is the core burden-of-disease measure."],
["Which statement best describes the current health status of Australians?",["Infectious disease is the main cause of death","Life expectancy is falling for most groups","Chronic disease is the main cause of death and illness","Males have a higher life expectancy than females"],2,"Chronic conditions such as CHD, dementia, cancer and COPD dominate both mortality and morbidity."],
["A sociological cause of risky health behaviour is:",["A genetic predisposition to high cholesterol","Peer norms that encourage binge drinking","A viral infection","Ageing of the arteries"],1,"Sociological causes come from social context (peers, culture, media, income), not biology."]],
sa:{q:"Analyse how sociological factors contribute to the risky health behaviours of Australians. Use examples to support your answer.",m:6,c:[["5–6","Clearly shows the relationship between a range of sociological factors and risky behaviours; uses relevant examples and data; logical, sustained response"],["3–4","Explains how some sociological factors influence risky behaviours with some examples"],["1–2","Identifies sociological factors and/or risky behaviours"]],pts:["Identifies factors such as SES, education, peers, culture/gender norms, media/marketing, geography","Links each factor to a specific behaviour (e.g. low SES → higher smoking; masculinity norms → risk-taking, late help-seeking)","Shows how factors interact (e.g. rural + low SES + limited services)","Uses data or trends (e.g. higher smoking and obesity rates in low-SES areas)","Draws an implication: behaviour is socially shaped, so strategies must target determinants, not just individuals"]}},

h2:{fa:"fa1",iq:0,v:"Analyse",t:"Analyse groups experiencing health inequities, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples and ONE other group",
inc:["Other group options: socioeconomically disadvantaged people, people living in rural and remote areas, culturally and linguistically diverse people, people with disability","What does the data show?","How do determinants interact to affect population groups?","Underlying causes of the inequity","Actions to improve the health of these groups"],
big:"Health inequities are not caused by one thing. They come from determinants that stack and interact, and the fix has to be led by and with the community.",
notes:[
["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples: what the data shows","Life expectancy for First Nations people is around **71.9 years for males and 75.6 years for females** (2020–22), a gap of about **8.8 years (males) and 8.1 years (females)**. The burden of disease is about **2.3 times** the rate of non-Indigenous Australians. The main contributors are mental and substance use disorders, cardiovascular disease, cancer, chronic kidney disease and diabetes. Infant mortality is about twice as high. There has been progress: infant mortality and smoking rates have fallen, and more children are enrolled in early education."],
["Underlying causes","The gap is rooted in **colonisation, dispossession, racism and intergenerational trauma**, including the Stolen Generations. These historical causes feed today's determinants: lower income and employment, overcrowded housing, lower educational attainment, distance from services and experiences of racism in health care. The determinants **interact**. For example, overcrowded housing spreads infection, which damages kidneys and hearts, and unemployment reduces access to healthy food and to services."],
["Actions that work","The **National Agreement on Closing the Gap (2020)** has 19 socioeconomic targets and four Priority Reforms: shared decision-making, building the community-controlled sector, transforming government organisations, and shared access to data. **Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisations (ACCHOs)** deliver culturally safe, holistic primary care. Other examples are the Indigenous health check (MBS item 715) and the Tackling Indigenous Smoking program. The principle is self-determination: programs designed **with**, not for, communities."],
["Second group example: rural and remote Australians","About **28% of Australians live outside major cities**. Death rates rise with remoteness: very remote areas have roughly **1.4 times** the death rate of major cities. These communities also have higher rates of road injury, suicide, smoking and risky drinking. The causes interact: fewer doctors and specialists, long travel times, lower incomes, more dangerous work (farming, mining), and a culture of stoicism. Responses include the **Royal Flying Doctor Service**, telehealth, rural generalist training, and incentives for health workers to move to the bush."],
["Second group example: low socioeconomic status","People in the lowest SES areas have **higher rates of chronic disease, smoking, obesity and avoidable deaths**, and live several years less than people in the highest SES areas. Income, education and employment shape health literacy, diet, housing quality and access to private care. Actions include bulk billing incentives, free preventive programs, the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme (PBS) safety net and community health centres."]],
con:[["Health inequity","A difference in health between groups that is unfair and avoidable, caused by social, economic and environmental conditions.","An 8-year life expectancy gap for First Nations peoples"],["Determinants of health","The factors that shape health: individual, sociocultural, socioeconomic and environmental.","Income, education, culture, access to services"],["Interaction of determinants","Determinants combine and strengthen each other, so disadvantage compounds.","Remote + low income + poor housing → much worse health"],["Self-determination","The right of First Nations communities to control the decisions and services that affect them.","ACCHOs run by local Aboriginal boards"],["Cultural safety","Care in which the patient feels respected and free from racism, as judged by the patient.","Aboriginal health workers present at appointments"]],
flow:["Colonisation & racism","Socioeconomic disadvantage","Poor access & housing","Risk factors","Chronic disease gap"],
sum:["First Nations life expectancy is about 8–9 years lower; the burden of disease is about 2.3 times higher.","Underlying causes are historical (colonisation, dispossession, racism, trauma) and flow into today's determinants.","Determinants interact and compound. Explain at least one chain of cause and effect in your answer.","What works is community-controlled, culturally safe and self-determined: Closing the Gap Priority Reforms and ACCHOs.","For your second group, know its data, causes and actions (e.g. rural/remote: the RFDS and telehealth)."],
tip:"Top responses show HOW determinants interact. Use a causal chain (A leads to B, which worsens C) rather than a list. Always pair a statistic with its underlying cause.",
reel:["h:Same country. *8 years* difference in life expectancy. Why?","s:≈8 yrs|life expectancy gap for First Nations Australians","p:It started with history|Colonisation, dispossession, racism and the Stolen Generations drive today's determinants.","p:Determinants stack|Overcrowded housing → infections → kidney and heart disease. That's an interaction.","l:What works|Community control (ACCHOs);Cultural safety;Closing the Gap reforms;Self-determination","c:Rural & remote|Fewer doctors;Longer travel;Riskier jobs||Response|Flying Doctors;Telehealth;Rural incentives","r:Data → Underlying cause → Interaction → Action"],
mcq:[["Which best explains why health inequities are 'inequities' rather than just differences?",["They are caused by genetics","They are unfair and avoidable","They only affect older people","They cannot be measured"],1,"Inequity specifically refers to differences that are unfair and avoidable, driven by determinants."],
["Which approach best reflects the Closing the Gap Priority Reforms?",["Government designs programs and delivers them to communities","Building the Aboriginal community-controlled sector","Reducing funding for remote clinics","Focusing only on individual behaviour change"],1,"Priority Reform 2 is building the community-controlled sector, which puts self-determination into practice."],
["Death rates for people in very remote areas compared with major cities are:",["Lower","About the same","About 1.4 times higher","10 times higher"],2,"Mortality rises with remoteness. Very remote death rates are about 1.4 times higher."]],
sa:{q:"Explain how the determinants of health interact to produce health inequities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.",m:5,c:[["5","Clearly explains how several determinants interact to produce inequities, with relevant examples/data"],["3–4","Explains how determinants influence the health of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, with some reference to interaction"],["1–2","Identifies determinants and/or inequities"]],pts:["Historical causes: colonisation, dispossession, racism, intergenerational trauma","Socioeconomic: income, employment, education → health literacy, access","Environmental: housing/overcrowding, remoteness, access to services","Sociocultural: cultural safety, trust in mainstream services","Interaction chain (e.g. overcrowding → rheumatic heart disease / kidney disease)","Data e.g. ≈8 year life expectancy gap, 2.3x burden of disease"]}},

h3:{fa:"fa1",iq:0,v:"Compare",t:"Compare Australia's health status with that of other OECD countries",
inc:["Where does Australia rank among OECD countries?","What are the reasons for Australia's ranking?","Why do some countries rank higher or lower?","What can Australia learn from other countries?"],
big:"Australia sits near the top of the OECD for life expectancy but falls behind on obesity and on the gaps within its own population. The lessons come from both directions.",
notes:[
["Where Australia ranks","The OECD (Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development) is a group of 38 mostly high-income countries. On **life expectancy, Australia is consistently in the top 10**, about 83 years compared with an OECD average of about 81. Australia is also better than average on deaths from heart disease and cancer survival, and has relatively low smoking rates. It ranks **poorly on adult obesity** (one of the highest OECD rates), **alcohol consumption** and the **gap between Indigenous and non-Indigenous health**."],
["Reasons for our ranking","Australia's strong ranking comes from **universal health coverage (Medicare)**, the PBS, world-leading tobacco control (plain packaging since 2012, high excise), vaccination programs, a high standard of living, clean water and sanitation, and road and workplace safety laws. Our weak areas come from sedentary, car-based lifestyles, energy-dense diets, cultural norms around alcohol, and persistent inequity."],
["Why do others rank higher or lower?","**Japan** ranks higher because of a traditional low-fat, fish- and vegetable-rich diet, very low obesity, strong community connection for older people, and universal health insurance. **Switzerland, Spain and Korea** also rank highly. The **United States** ranks lower despite spending the most per person. It has no universal coverage, high obesity, high rates of gun and drug-overdose deaths, and large inequities in access to care. So spending more does not automatically produce better health; how the money is used matters."],
["Lessons for Australia","From **Japan**: support healthy ageing and community food cultures. From **Nordic countries**: invest in prevention and a strong primary-care system, and reduce income inequality. From **countries with sugar taxes** (UK, Mexico): fiscal policy can change diets. From **Portugal**: treating drug use as a health issue rather than a crime. The US is a warning about how costly and inequitable a health system without universal coverage can be."]],
con:[["OECD","A group of 38 mostly high-income countries used as the comparison set for health statistics.","Australia, Japan, Canada, UK, USA"],["Universal health coverage","Everyone can access needed health services without financial hardship.","Medicare in Australia; Japan's insurance system"],["Health-adjusted comparison","Comparing on several measures, not just one, because rankings vary by indicator.","Top 10 for life expectancy; bottom third for obesity"],["Fiscal policy","Using taxes and subsidies to change health behaviour.","Tobacco excise; sugar taxes overseas"]],
sum:["Australia is in the top 10 of the OECD for life expectancy (≈83 years vs an OECD average of ≈81).","Strengths: Medicare, the PBS, tobacco control, vaccination and a high standard of living.","Weaknesses: high obesity, high alcohol use and large internal inequities.","Japan ranks higher (diet, low obesity, social cohesion); the USA ranks lower despite the highest spending.","Lessons: prevention, sugar taxes, healthy ageing and treating drug use as a health issue."],
tip:"'Compare' means show similarities AND differences. Use comparative language (whereas, similarly, in contrast) and give a reason for each difference.",
reel:["h:We spend *less* than the USA on health, yet we live *4 years longer*. How?","s:Top 10|Australia's OECD ranking for life expectancy","c:Australia ✓|Medicare;Tobacco control;Vaccination||Australia ✗|Obesity;Alcohol;Inequity","p:Japan beats us|Fish-and-veg diet, low obesity and older people who stay socially connected.","p:USA lags behind|Highest spending, but no universal coverage, high obesity and big inequities.","l:Steal these ideas|Sugar taxes;Stronger prevention;Healthy ageing;Drug use as health","r:Rank → Reason → Who does better → Lesson"],
mcq:[["Which country typically has higher life expectancy than Australia?",["United States","Japan","Mexico","Hungary"],1,"Japan consistently ranks at or near the top of the OECD for life expectancy."],
["Australia performs relatively POORLY compared with other OECD countries on:",["Smoking rates","Adult obesity","Cancer survival","Universal coverage"],1,"Australia has one of the higher rates of adult obesity in the OECD."],
["The US example shows that:",["Higher spending always produces better health","Universal coverage reduces life expectancy","Health spending alone does not guarantee better health status","Obesity has no effect on mortality"],2,"The US spends the most per person but has lower life expectancy, so how money is spent matters."]],
sa:{q:"Compare Australia's health status with that of ONE other OECD country. In your answer, account for the differences.",m:6,c:[["5–6","Provides detailed similarities AND differences in health status using relevant data; accounts for differences with clear reasons"],["3–4","Describes similarities and/or differences with some reasons"],["1–2","Provides some general information about health status of Australia and/or another country"]],pts:["Comparison measures: life expectancy, obesity, mortality, health spending","Similarities (e.g. both have chronic disease as main burden)","Differences with data (e.g. Japan ≈84–85 vs Australia ≈83)","Reasons: diet, health system, policy, culture, inequity","Comparative language throughout"]}},

h4:{fa:"fa1",iq:0,v:"Examine",t:"Examine cardiovascular disease, cancer and ONE other condition in Australia using Australia's Health and other reports",
inc:["Mortality and morbidity, prevalence and incidence","Risk factors and protective factors","Where and for whom are these conditions changing?"],
big:"CVD deaths have fallen dramatically, cancer survival keeps improving and diabetes is rising. Each trend has a reason you can name.",
notes:[
["Cardiovascular disease (CVD)","CVD covers coronary heart disease (CHD), stroke, heart failure and peripheral vascular disease. It causes about **1 in 4 deaths** in Australia, and CHD is the leading single cause of death for males. The good news: **CVD death rates have fallen by more than 80% since 1968**. The reasons are lower smoking rates, better control of blood pressure and cholesterol, statins, stents and bypass surgery, ambulance and emergency care, and public awareness campaigns. CVD is still more common in males, older people, First Nations peoples, low-SES groups and people in remote areas."],
["CVD risk and protective factors","**Modifiable risk factors:** smoking, high blood pressure, high cholesterol, physical inactivity, overweight and obesity, poor diet (high salt, saturated fat), excess alcohol, diabetes. **Non-modifiable:** age, male sex, family history, ethnicity. **Protective factors:** regular physical activity, a diet rich in fruit, vegetables and wholegrains, a healthy weight, not smoking, regular health checks (e.g. Heart Health Checks), and social connection."],
["Cancer","Cancer is the **largest contributor to burden of disease**. About **1 in 2 Australians will be diagnosed by age 85**, with about 165,000 new cases a year. The most commonly diagnosed are prostate, breast, melanoma, bowel and lung. **Lung cancer causes the most cancer deaths.** **Incidence has risen** mainly because the population is ageing and screening detects more cancers. But **mortality rates have fallen** and **5-year survival is now about 70%**. Risk factors include tobacco, UV exposure, alcohol, obesity, physical inactivity, poor diet, age and genetics. Protective factors include sun protection (Slip Slop Slap Seek Slide), HPV vaccination, not smoking, and the national screening programs: bowel (ages 50–74, from 45 on request), breast (BreastScreen), cervical (HPV test every 5 years) and the new lung cancer screening program (2025)."],
["Third condition: diabetes","About **1.3 million Australians (≈5%)** have known diabetes, and about **85–90% of them have type 2**. Prevalence has more than doubled since the late 1980s. That rise is linked to obesity, physical inactivity, poor diet and an ageing population. Diabetes is about **3–4 times more common among First Nations adults** and is higher in low-SES areas. It is a major cause of heart disease, kidney failure, blindness and amputations. Protective factors: a healthy weight, regular physical activity, a diet high in fibre and low in added sugar, and early screening (AUSDRISK). The National Diabetes Services Scheme (NDSS) supports people with diabetes."],
["Prevalence vs incidence","**Prevalence** is the total number of people living with a condition at a point in time. **Incidence** is the number of NEW cases in a period. Cancer incidence can rise while mortality falls (better detection, better treatment). Prevalence can rise even when incidence is steady, because people survive longer with the condition."]],
con:[["Prevalence","Total number of cases (existing + new) at a point in time.","≈1.3 million Australians living with diabetes"],["Incidence","Number of NEW cases diagnosed in a set period.","≈165,000 new cancer diagnoses per year"],["Risk factor","Something that increases the chance of developing a disease.","Smoking → lung cancer and CVD"],["Protective factor","Something that decreases the chance of developing a disease.","Regular physical activity"],["Screening","Testing people without symptoms to detect disease early.","National Bowel Cancer Screening Program (50–74)"]],
sum:["CVD causes ≈1 in 4 deaths, but death rates have fallen by more than 80% since 1968 (less smoking, better treatment, prevention).","Cancer is the biggest contributor to burden. Incidence is rising, but mortality is falling and survival is ≈70%.","Lung cancer causes the most cancer deaths; prostate and breast are the most commonly diagnosed.","Diabetes (mostly type 2) is rising, linked to obesity, inactivity and ageing; it is 3–4x higher in First Nations adults.","Know the difference: prevalence (all cases) vs incidence (new cases)."],
tip:"For 'examine', inquire into the patterns AND the reasons. Use trend language: 'has declined since…', 'is higher among…'. A strong response names WHO is most affected and WHY.",
reel:["h:Heart disease deaths have dropped by *80%*. Here's why that matters.","s:80%↓|fall in CVD death rates since 1968","l:Why CVD fell|Less smoking;BP and cholesterol meds;Stents and surgery;Faster emergency care","p:Cancer paradox|More cancers diagnosed (ageing + screening), but fewer deaths and ≈70% survival.","s:1 in 2|Australians diagnosed with cancer by 85","p:Diabetes is climbing|Obesity + inactivity + ageing. 3–4x higher in First Nations adults.","c:Prevalence|ALL current cases||Incidence|NEW cases only","r:Trend + reason + who's most affected"],
mcq:[["Cancer incidence in Australia has increased mainly because of:",["Reduced screening","Population ageing and improved detection","Increased smoking rates","Falling survival rates"],1,"An older population and more screening mean more cancers are found, even as death rates fall."],
["Which cancer causes the most deaths in Australia?",["Breast cancer","Prostate cancer","Lung cancer","Melanoma"],2,"Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death, largely due to tobacco."],
["'The number of new cases of a disease in a given period' defines:",["Prevalence","Incidence","Mortality","Morbidity"],1,"Incidence = new cases. Prevalence = all existing cases."]],
sa:{q:"Examine the trends in cardiovascular disease in Australia and the factors that have contributed to these trends.",m:6,c:[["5–6","Detailed examination of CVD trends using data; clearly links multiple factors (prevention, treatment, behaviour) to the trends; identifies groups most affected"],["3–4","Describes CVD trends and some contributing factors"],["1–2","Provides general information about CVD"]],pts:["Mortality fallen >80% since 1968 but still ≈1 in 4 deaths","Factors: reduced smoking, BP/cholesterol control, medical technology, emergency care, awareness","Risk factors still prevalent: obesity, inactivity, diet","Higher in males, older people, First Nations, low SES, remote","Prevalence persists because people survive longer"]}},

h5:{fa:"fa1",iq:0,v:"Investigate",t:"Investigate the impact of an ageing population on Australia's health",
inc:["What does the data show?","What is healthy ageing?","Opportunities and challenges of an ageing population","Current and future strategies to support healthy ageing","What must government and non-government agencies consider with a growing and ageing population?"],
big:"An ageing population is a success story, not a crisis. The challenge is making sure the extra years are healthy, connected and well supported.",
notes:[
["What the data shows","About **17% of Australians are aged 65 and over** (≈4.4 million), and this is projected to reach about **22–23% by 2066**. The number aged **85+ is growing fastest**. Older Australians are living longer and in better health than earlier generations. Most rate their health as good to excellent. However, older age brings higher rates of chronic disease, multimorbidity, dementia (the leading cause of death for women), falls, sensory loss and a greater need for health and aged care. People aged 65+ account for a large share of hospital use and PBS spending."],
["What healthy ageing is","The World Health Organization defines **healthy ageing** as 'the process of developing and maintaining the functional ability that enables wellbeing in older age'. It is not just the absence of disease. It means older people can meet their basic needs, keep learning and making decisions, stay mobile, build relationships and contribute to society. Physical activity (including strength and balance training), social connection, good nutrition, lifelong learning and age-friendly environments all support it."],
["Opportunities","Older people are a **resource**. They volunteer (contributing billions of dollars in unpaid work), provide grandparent childcare, carry knowledge and experience, keep working longer, and drive a growing 'silver economy'. For example, about a quarter of older Australians volunteer."],
["Challenges","**Health system demand:** more chronic disease and complex care. **Aged care:** the 2021 Royal Commission found substandard care and neglect. **Workforce:** shortages of carers, nurses and GPs. **Carers:** many are unpaid family members, often older women themselves. **Economic pressure:** a smaller tax base relative to the number of retirees (the Intergenerational Report). **Social:** isolation, loneliness and elder abuse."],
["Strategies, now and future","Current: My Aged Care, the Home Care Packages program and the new Support at Home program, the new Aged Care Act (2024) and its rights-based approach, Healthy Ageing grants, falls prevention programs, the National Dementia Strategy, superannuation and the Age Pension. Future planning must consider workforce growth, **ageing in place** (support at home rather than residential care), digital health and telehealth for older people, age-friendly cities (links to SDG 11), and prevention across the whole life course, because the healthy ageing of tomorrow's 70-year-olds starts with today's teenagers."]],
con:[["Healthy ageing","Developing and maintaining the functional ability that enables wellbeing in older age (WHO).","A 78-year-old who walks daily, volunteers and lives independently"],["Ageing in place","Supporting older people to stay living safely at home and in their community.","Home Care Packages, home modifications"],["Multimorbidity","Living with two or more chronic conditions at the same time.","Arthritis + diabetes + heart disease"],["Dependency ratio","The number of people not of working age compared with the number of working age, which affects funding.","Fewer taxpayers for each retiree"]],
sum:["≈17% of Australians are 65+, rising to ≈22–23% by 2066; the 85+ group is growing fastest.","Healthy ageing (WHO) means maintaining functional ability for wellbeing, not just avoiding disease.","Opportunities: volunteering, caring, knowledge and experience, extended working lives, the economy.","Challenges: chronic disease, demand on aged care, workforce shortages, carer burden, cost, isolation.","Strategies: Support at Home, the new Aged Care Act, falls prevention, dementia strategy, ageing in place, age-friendly cities."],
tip:"'Investigate' means plan, inquire into and draw conclusions. Balance challenges AND opportunities, and finish with a judgement about what agencies must prioritise.",
reel:["h:By 2066, almost *1 in 4* Australians will be over 65. Problem or opportunity?","s:17%|of Australians are aged 65+ today","p:Healthy ageing|Not 'no disease'. It's keeping the ability to do what you value.","c:Opportunities|Volunteering;Childcare;Experience||Challenges|Chronic disease;Aged care;Workforce","l:Strategies|Ageing in place;Falls prevention;Dementia strategy;Age-friendly cities","p:Plan ahead|Agencies must think about workforce, funding, carers and digital access.","r:Data → Healthy ageing → Opp vs Challenge → Strategy"],
mcq:[["According to the WHO, healthy ageing is best described as:",["Living to 100 years","Having no chronic diseases","Maintaining functional ability that enables wellbeing","Living in residential aged care"],2,"WHO focuses on functional ability, not the absence of disease."],
["Which is an OPPORTUNITY of an ageing population?",["Increased demand for aged care beds","Increased volunteering and grandparent childcare","Workforce shortages in nursing","Rising PBS costs"],1,"Older people contribute a great deal through volunteering and unpaid care."],
["'Ageing in place' refers to:",["Moving older people to residential care early","Supporting older people to live at home in their community","Older people working past 70","Retirement villages only"],1,"Ageing in place keeps people at home with support, which is usually preferred and less costly."]],
sa:{q:"Investigate the challenges AND opportunities that an ageing population presents for the Australian health system.",m:8,c:[["7–8","Comprehensive investigation of both challenges and opportunities; uses relevant data; draws a clear, supported conclusion"],["5–6","Explains challenges and opportunities with some data/examples"],["3–4","Describes challenges and/or opportunities"],["1–2","Identifies features of an ageing population"]],pts:["Data: ≈17% 65+, rising to ≈22–23%; 85+ fastest growing","Challenges: chronic disease/multimorbidity, aged care demand, workforce, carers, cost","Opportunities: volunteering, childcare, knowledge, silver economy, longer working lives","Strategies: Support at Home, Aged Care Act, falls prevention, dementia","Conclusion/judgement about planning priorities"]}},

h6:{fa:"fa1",iq:1,v:"Assess",t:"Assess the effectiveness of the healthcare system in Australia",
inc:["The role of the healthcare system","Equity of access","Future opportunities"],
big:"Australia's system delivers good outcomes at reasonable cost, but access is not equal. Your job in an 'assess' answer is to weigh strengths against weaknesses and make a judgement.",
notes:[
["The role of the system","The healthcare system aims to **promote health, prevent illness, diagnose and treat disease, rehabilitate and provide palliative care**. It runs on two levels. **Primary care** (GPs, pharmacists, allied health, community health) is the first point of contact. **Secondary and tertiary care** (specialists and hospitals) follows. It is a **mixed public-private system**: Medicare provides universal coverage, while private health insurance adds choice."],
["Strengths","Universal access through **Medicare** (since 1984) and subsidised medicines through the **PBS**. High life expectancy and good cancer survival. Australia's system is regularly ranked among the best performing in the world (e.g. Commonwealth Fund comparisons put it near the top for health outcomes and equity). Health spending of about **10% of GDP** is moderate by OECD standards, and public hospital treatment is free."],
["Weaknesses: equity of access","Access is **not equal**. There are long **elective surgery waiting times** in public hospitals, falling **bulk billing** rates that bring out-of-pocket costs (≈1 in 3 people delay seeing a GP or specialist because of cost in some surveys), **dental care** that sits mostly outside Medicare, and a shortage of mental health services. There are fewer doctors in rural and remote areas, and First Nations peoples face barriers of cultural safety. The system is **fragmented**: the Commonwealth funds GPs, the states run hospitals, and patients fall through the gaps between them. It remains weighted towards treatment rather than prevention, which gets only ≈2% of spending."],
["Future opportunities","Strengthening primary care (**Medicare Urgent Care Clinics**, triple bulk-billing incentives, MyMedicare voluntary patient registration), **digital health** (telehealth, My Health Record, e-prescriptions), expanding pharmacist and nurse practitioner roles, rural workforce incentives, greater investment in prevention (National Preventive Health Strategy target: 5% of spending by 2030) and person-centred models of care."]],
con:[["Effectiveness","How well the system achieves its goals: outcomes, access, efficiency and equity.","High life expectancy, but long waiting lists"],["Equity of access","Everyone can access the care they need regardless of income, location or background.","Bulk billing improves access for low-income patients"],["Primary care","The first point of contact with the health system, in the community.","GP, pharmacist, community health centre"],["Out-of-pocket costs","Costs paid by the patient that are not covered by Medicare or insurance.","Specialist gap fees, dental care"]],
sum:["The system's role covers promotion, prevention, treatment, rehabilitation and palliation, delivered through primary → secondary → tertiary care.","Strengths: universal Medicare + PBS, strong outcomes, moderate cost (≈10% of GDP), free public hospitals.","Weaknesses: waiting lists, falling bulk billing, out-of-pocket costs, dental gaps, a rural workforce shortage, fragmentation.","Prevention gets only ≈2% of spending, so the system is weighted towards treatment.","Future: Urgent Care Clinics, bulk-billing incentives, digital health, a bigger role for pharmacists and nurse practitioners."],
tip:"'Assess' needs a JUDGEMENT. Open with a clear position (e.g. 'largely effective but inequitable'), weigh the evidence on both sides, then return to the judgement in your conclusion.",
reel:["h:Is our healthcare system actually *good*? Let's assess it.","p:The role|Promote health, prevent and treat illness, rehabilitate, and care at end of life.","c:Strengths|Medicare;PBS;High life expectancy||Weaknesses|Waiting lists;Out-of-pocket costs;Rural gaps","s:≈2%|of health spending goes to prevention","l:Future fixes|Urgent Care Clinics;Bulk billing incentives;Telehealth;Prevention funding","p:Judgement|Effective overall, but not yet equitable for everyone.","r:Assess = weigh it up + make a CALL"],
mcq:[["Which best describes Australia's healthcare system?",["Fully private","Fully public","A mixed public-private system with universal coverage","Insurance-only"],2,"Medicare gives universal coverage; private insurance and services sit alongside it."],
["A key equity-of-access weakness of the Australian system is:",["Free public hospital care","Out-of-pocket costs and declining bulk billing","The PBS","Universal coverage"],1,"Out-of-pocket costs stop some people seeing a GP or specialist, especially low-income people."],
["Approximately what proportion of health spending goes to prevention?",["≈2%","≈20%","≈40%","≈60%"],0,"Prevention receives only about 2% of spending, which shows a treatment-focused system."]],
sa:{q:"Assess the effectiveness of Australia's healthcare system in providing equity of access for all Australians.",m:8,c:[["7–8","Makes an informed judgement about effectiveness; balances strengths and weaknesses of access with relevant examples/data; considers different population groups"],["5–6","Explains strengths and weaknesses of the healthcare system relating to access, with some judgement"],["3–4","Describes features of the healthcare system and access"],["1–2","Identifies features of the healthcare system"]],pts:["Clear judgement statement","Strengths: Medicare, PBS, public hospitals, bulk billing, PBS safety net","Weaknesses: waiting lists, out-of-pocket costs, rural workforce, dental, mental health, cultural safety","Groups: low SES, rural/remote, First Nations","Future opportunities & conclusion that revisits the judgement"]}},

h7:{fa:"fa1",iq:1,v:"Explain",t:"Explain how government and non-government organisations share responsibility for the health system",
inc:["Federal, state/territory and local government","Non-government sector (private and not-for-profit)"],
big:"Health in Australia is a shared job: Canberra funds and sets policy, the states run hospitals, councils keep communities safe, and NGOs fill gaps and advocate.",
notes:[
["Federal (Commonwealth) government","The federal government **funds and sets national policy**. It runs **Medicare** (MBS) and the **PBS**, subsidises private health insurance (the rebate), funds aged care, regulates medicines through the **TGA**, funds a large share of public hospitals via agreements with the states, runs national health promotion campaigns and screening programs, and collects and reports national data through the **AIHW**. The Department of Health, Disability and Ageing leads this work."],
["State and territory governments","The states **run public hospitals** (through Local Health Districts in NSW), ambulance services, community and mental health services, school dental and immunisation programs, public health regulation (food safety, disease notification) and the registration of facilities. In NSW, **NSW Health** is responsible."],
["Local government","Councils look after **environmental and community health**: waste collection, sanitation, food premises inspections, immunisation clinics, parks, bike paths and recreation facilities, local health promotion and planning (e.g. smoke-free areas, shade)."],
["Non-government organisations (NGOs)","**Private** providers include private hospitals, private health insurers, GPs (who are private businesses mostly funded through Medicare), pharmacies, and allied health. **Not-for-profit** organisations include the **Heart Foundation, Cancer Council, Diabetes Australia, Beyond Blue, Black Dog Institute and the Royal Flying Doctor Service**, as well as ACCHOs. They provide services, research, advocacy, education, support groups and fundraising, and often reach groups governments find hard to reach."],
["Why share responsibility?","No single body can do everything. Sharing allows **national consistency** (federal), **local delivery** (state and local) and **specialist expertise and reach** (NGOs). The downside is **fragmentation and cost-shifting**. Patients can fall between Commonwealth-funded GP care and state-run hospitals, which is why collaboration and person-centred care matter (next dot point)."]],
con:[["Commonwealth role","National funding and policy: Medicare, PBS, aged care, TGA, AIHW.","PBS subsidises medicines"],["State/territory role","Running public hospitals, ambulance, community and mental health services.","NSW Health and Local Health Districts"],["Local government role","Environmental health and community facilities.","Food premises inspections, bike paths"],["Not-for-profit NGO","An organisation that puts its income back into its health mission.","Cancer Council, Heart Foundation"]],
sum:["Federal: funds and sets policy. Medicare, PBS, aged care, TGA, AIHW, national campaigns.","States/territories: deliver services. Public hospitals, ambulance, community and mental health.","Local: environmental health. Sanitation, food inspection, parks, immunisation clinics.","NGOs (private and not-for-profit): services, research, advocacy, support (e.g. Cancer Council, RFDS).","Sharing gives reach and expertise, but brings fragmentation and cost-shifting."],
tip:"'Explain' means show cause and effect: say WHAT each level does AND WHY that arrangement matters for health outcomes. A table-style structure (level → role → example) helps.",
reel:["h:Who actually *runs* health in Australia? Spoiler: everyone.","p:Federal|Funds and sets the rules: Medicare, PBS, aged care, the TGA.","p:State|Runs the hospitals and ambulances. In NSW, that's NSW Health.","p:Local council|Clean water, food inspections, parks and bike paths.","l:NGOs|Cancer Council;Heart Foundation;Beyond Blue;Flying Doctors","c:Upside|Reach + expertise||Downside|Fragmentation","r:Level → Role → Example → Why it matters"],
mcq:[["Which level of government is primarily responsible for running public hospitals?",["Federal","State/territory","Local","Non-government"],1,"States and territories run public hospitals, with substantial federal funding."],
["The Therapeutic Goods Administration (TGA), which regulates medicines, is part of:",["Local government","State government","The federal government","A not-for-profit organisation"],2,"The TGA is a Commonwealth regulator."],
["Which is a not-for-profit NGO?",["Medicare","NSW Health","Cancer Council","Local council"],2,"Cancer Council is a not-for-profit providing research, advocacy and support."]],
sa:{q:"Explain how government and non-government organisations share responsibility for health in Australia. Use examples.",m:6,c:[["5–6","Clearly explains the roles of all levels of government AND non-government organisations, showing how responsibility is shared, with relevant examples"],["3–4","Describes the roles of some levels of government and/or NGOs with examples"],["1–2","Identifies some health organisations or roles"]],pts:["Federal: Medicare, PBS, TGA, funding","State: hospitals, ambulance, community health","Local: environmental health, facilities","NGOs: private hospitals/insurers; not-for-profits e.g. Cancer Council","How sharing works / why it matters (reach, expertise, fragmentation)"]}},

h8:{fa:"fa1",iq:1,v:"Outline",t:"Outline how government and non-government organisations collaborate to provide person-centred healthcare",
inc:[],
big:"Person-centred care puts the patient at the centre: services work together around the person rather than making the person work around the system.",
notes:[
["What is person-centred care?","**Person-centred care** treats each person as a partner in their own care. It respects their preferences, culture and values, involves families and carers, and coordinates services so the person gets seamless care. It is one of the principles of the Australian Charter of Healthcare Rights and of the national safety and quality standards."],
["How organisations collaborate","**Primary Health Networks (PHNs)** are 31 federally funded, independent regions. They commission local services and link GPs, hospitals and community organisations. **Headspace** is federally funded and delivered by local NGO lead agencies, bringing mental health, physical health, alcohol and other drug, and work and study support for 12–25 year olds into one place. **ACCHOs** receive government funding but are run by communities to deliver holistic care. **Cancer care teams** combine public hospitals, Cancer Council support lines and community nurses. The **NDIS** links government funding with private and not-for-profit providers chosen by the participant. **Medicare Urgent Care Clinics** are federally funded and run in partnership with states, PHNs and private GP clinics."],
["Why it matters","Collaboration reduces **duplication and gaps**, improves **continuity of care**, makes services easier to navigate, and lets the person shape their own care. Shared tools like **My Health Record** support it by letting different providers see the same information."]],
con:[["Person-centred care","Care that respects the person's preferences, needs and values and involves them in decisions.","A care plan built with the patient and their family"],["Primary Health Network (PHN)","Regional bodies that coordinate and commission local health services.","31 PHNs across Australia"],["headspace","A youth mental health service combining government funding and NGO delivery.","One-stop shop for 12–25 year olds"],["Continuity of care","A smooth, connected experience across different providers over time.","GP, hospital and community nurse share a care plan"]],
sum:["Person-centred care respects the person's preferences, culture and values and involves them in decisions.","Primary Health Networks link GPs, hospitals and community services in each region.","headspace = federal funding + NGO delivery, a one-stop shop for young people.","ACCHOs, the NDIS and Urgent Care Clinics are other examples of collaboration.","Collaboration reduces gaps and duplication and improves continuity; My Health Record supports it."],
tip:"'Outline' is a lower-order verb: give a brief sketch of the main features. Name 2–3 clear examples of collaboration rather than writing at length. Watch the mark value.",
reel:["h:What if the health system worked *around you*, not the other way round?","p:Person-centred care|Your preferences, culture and values guide decisions about your care.","p:headspace|Federal $$ + local NGO = one-stop youth mental health.","l:Other collaborations|Primary Health Networks;ACCHOs;NDIS;Urgent Care Clinics","p:The glue|My Health Record lets every provider see the same story.","r:Who funds + who delivers + how it centres the person"],
mcq:[["Primary Health Networks (PHNs) mainly:",["Run public hospitals","Coordinate and commission local health services","Sell private insurance","Regulate medicines"],1,"PHNs coordinate and commission services to fill local gaps."],
["headspace is an example of collaboration because it:",["Is entirely privately funded","Combines government funding with NGO service delivery","Is run by local councils only","Only treats physical injuries"],1,"It is federally funded and delivered by local not-for-profit lead agencies."],
["Person-centred care means:",["The doctor makes all decisions","Care is organised around the person's needs and preferences","The cheapest option is always chosen","Hospitals are the first point of contact"],1,"The person is a partner in care, so services are organised around them."]],
sa:{q:"Outline how government and non-government organisations collaborate to provide person-centred healthcare.",m:4,c:[["4","Outlines person-centred care and provides clear examples of collaboration between government and NGOs"],["2–3","Describes some collaboration between organisations"],["1","Identifies an organisation or feature of person-centred care"]],pts:["Definition of person-centred care","Example 1 (e.g. headspace)","Example 2 (e.g. PHNs / ACCHOs)","Benefit: continuity, fewer gaps"]}},

h9:{fa:"fa1",iq:1,v:"Discuss",t:"Discuss health expenditure and its impact on current and future populations",
inc:["Healthcare versus prevention","Sustainability, access and equity","Medicare, private health insurance and related Commonwealth-funded programs"],
big:"Australia spends around 10% of GDP on health. The real debate is WHAT the money goes to (treatment or prevention) and whether that can last as the population ages.",
notes:[
["The numbers and trends","Australia spends about **$250 billion a year on health (≈10% of GDP)**, and spending is rising faster than population growth. Roughly **two-thirds comes from governments** (the Commonwealth ≈40%+, states and territories ≈25–30%) and the rest from **individuals (out-of-pocket ≈15%), private insurers and others**. The biggest areas of spending are **public hospitals, primary care, and medicines**. The drivers are an ageing population, chronic disease, new technologies and drugs, and rising community expectations."],
["Healthcare (treatment) vs prevention","Most spending goes to **treating illness**. Only about **2% goes to prevention**, compared with around 3% or more in countries like Canada and the UK. Yet prevention is highly cost-effective. Tobacco control, immunisation and screening return several dollars for every dollar spent. The **National Preventive Health Strategy 2021–2030** aims to raise prevention to **5% of spending by 2030**. The dilemma: treatment produces visible results now, while prevention pays off years later, which is a political challenge."],
["Medicare, PHI and related programs","**Medicare** gives free public hospital care and subsidises GP and specialist services through the MBS. It is funded through general taxation plus the **Medicare levy (2%)**. The **PBS** subsidises over 900 medicines, with safety nets. **Private health insurance** is encouraged through the **30% (means-tested) rebate**, the **Medicare Levy Surcharge** on higher earners without cover, and **Lifetime Health Cover** loading for people who join after 30. About **45% of Australians hold hospital cover**. Related programs include the NDIS, aged care, PBS safety nets and Closing the Gap PBS co-payments."],
["Impact on current and future populations","**Sustainability:** an ageing population and a smaller tax base put pressure on funding (Intergenerational Report). **Access and equity:** rising out-of-pocket costs mean low-income people delay care, while PHI subsidies are criticised for mainly benefiting wealthier people. **Future generations:** under-investing in prevention now means more chronic disease, and more cost, later. Spending choices today shape the health of tomorrow's population."]],
con:[["Health expenditure","Total spending on health goods and services by governments, individuals and insurers.","≈$250 billion (≈10% of GDP)"],["Medicare levy","A 2% tax on taxable income that helps fund Medicare.","Paid by most taxpayers"],["Medicare Levy Surcharge","An extra tax on higher earners without private hospital cover.","Encourages PHI uptake"],["Sustainability","The ability to keep funding the system long-term without excluding people.","An ageing population increases demand"],["Cost-effectiveness","Health gain per dollar spent.","Immunisation saves more than it costs"]],
sum:["≈$250 billion a year (≈10% of GDP); two-thirds government funded, and rising.","Treatment dominates; prevention is only ≈2%, with a target of 5% by 2030.","Medicare (levy + tax), the PBS, and PHI incentives (rebate, MLS, Lifetime Health Cover).","Out-of-pocket costs threaten equity; PHI subsidies tend to favour wealthier people.","Ageing and chronic disease threaten sustainability. Prevention now = savings and better health later."],
tip:"'Discuss' = points FOR and AGAINST. Present both sides of the treatment vs prevention debate and of private health insurance, then reach a reasoned position.",
reel:["h:$250 *billion* a year on health. Where does it all go?","s:≈10%|of GDP spent on health","c:Treatment|≈98% of spending;Results now||Prevention|≈2% of spending;Pays off later","p:The target|5% on prevention by 2030 (National Preventive Health Strategy).","l:The PHI carrot & stick|30% rebate;Medicare Levy Surcharge;Lifetime Health Cover","p:Future risk|Ageing + chronic disease + small tax base = a sustainability squeeze.","r:Discuss = both sides + a reasoned view"],
mcq:[["Approximately what percentage of GDP does Australia spend on health?",["≈2%","≈5%","≈10%","≈20%"],2,"Australia spends roughly 10% of GDP on health, moderate for the OECD."],
["Lifetime Health Cover loading is designed to:",["Fund public hospitals directly","Encourage people to take out private hospital insurance before age 31","Pay for the PBS","Reduce Medicare levy for low incomes"],1,"People who join after 30 pay a loading, which encourages earlier uptake."],
["Which is an argument FOR increasing prevention spending?",["It produces immediate visible results","It is often highly cost-effective and reduces future chronic disease","It reduces the need for any hospitals","It only benefits private patients"],1,"Prevention (e.g. immunisation, tobacco control) returns more than it costs over time."]],
sa:{q:"Discuss the impact of health expenditure on the sustainability and equity of Australia's healthcare system for current and future populations.",m:8,c:[["7–8","Presents a well-reasoned discussion of points for and/or against, linking expenditure to sustainability AND equity, for current and future populations, with relevant data"],["5–6","Explains the impact of expenditure on sustainability and/or equity with some examples"],["3–4","Describes aspects of health expenditure"],["1–2","Identifies features of health expenditure"]],pts:["≈10% GDP, rising; funding sources","Treatment vs prevention (≈2%, target 5%)","Medicare, PBS, PHI incentives and their equity effects","Out-of-pocket costs; low-income access","Future: ageing, chronic disease, IGR sustainability; position reached"]}}
};
